From 329c9603411f94a40aad71c9b5e48ea7cd3bf0e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 20:37:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/27] Short signals were unexecutable; gate them to the F&O
 reality
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every engine here asks whether a trade is good. None asked the question that
comes first for a retail Indian cash account: can it hold the position at all?
For the SHORT side the honest answer is usually no. The cash segment has no
overnight delivery short — a bearish view for the multi-week horizons these
signals describe is only holdable as a single-stock future, which lists on ~180
names, trades in whole lots, and after SEBI's ~Rs 15L minimum contract value
often costs more per lot than a personal book can commit. The autopilot's own
comment already knew this ('shorting equity delivery overnight is not available
to Indian retail') while the book went on opening exactly those shorts and
booking fictional P&L on them.

engine/india_market.py is the executability layer between synthesis and sizing.
A short on a non-F&O name is now failed outright with the real reason; a short on
an F&O name is re-sized in whole futures lots, held on ~22% margin, and costed on
the futures statutory stack (STT sell-side only, lighter charges) instead of the
delivery stack. A small book discovers it cannot take even one lot, which is the
true state of the world and was previously hidden.

Also corrected the cost model's two blind spots that most distort a SMALL
account: 18% GST on exchange+SEBI charges, and the flat per-sell DP charge that
is percentage-invisible on a large ticket and dominant on a tiny one. A purely
proportional model systematically flattered the small books this system is for.

Executability travels with the dossier too, so the calibration ledger never
scores a bearish call as a trade that could not have been opened.
