Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The rearchitecture made the served views fetch the market live, but the cron
pipeline still ran the old bulk-ingestion stages every day — price and OHLC
pulls, macro, NSE archives, the columnar panel rebuild, retention prune, and the
full-panel base-rate studies. Every one of those is a WRITE (or a full-panel
READ) against the metered database, so the exact egress this whole change exists
to eliminate was still being spent on a schedule, quietly, behind a snapshot that
now came from Yahoo anyway.

A `dstage` wrapper marks the bulk-DATA stages and skips them outright when live
mode is on, reporting the skip rather than hiding it. What still runs is only the
tiny, essential work: sync the universe metadata, register the day's forecasts,
score due claims, rebuild the snapshot from live data, run autopilot on the paper
book. Base-rate studies — which need a deep survivorship-corrected panel that is
deliberately no longer stored — are served from their last computed records and
refreshed on demand, not daily. The cron's footprint on Neon goes from the
system's largest recurring transfer to essentially nothing.
