From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 21:33:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/27] Surface whether the book can actually afford the
 strategy
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

capital_feasibility was fully implemented and called from nowhere — a hard,
grounded constraint sitting as dead code. Indian equity has no fractional shares,
so a book below a threshold size cannot buy a whole share of most of the ranked
names and silently drifts into a cheap-stock tilt that carries none of this
system's evidence. That binds BEFORE alpha, tax or slippage, and it was being
discovered only through underperformance.

Every candidate scan now reports it: what fraction of the universe the book can
afford at the intended breadth, and the capital that would make the strategy
executable. For a small personal account the honest answer is often 'paper-trade
while the forward record accumulates; a broad index ETF diversifies better per
rupee' — and now the system says so instead of quietly recommending names the
account can't hold.
