From 9785978f89ee655d82f9452d108354d659e6a6e3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 20:52:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/27] Risk-managed momentum: forecast the crash, cut the
 sleeve before it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Momentum is this platform's primary measured edge and carries one ruinous flaw —
rare, violent crashes in panic rebounds (2009: momentum fell ~-73% in a quarter),
where the losers it is not short rip up together and the winners it holds stall.
Barroso & Santa-Clara (2015) showed these crashes are forecastable by momentum's
OWN realized volatility, and that scaling the sleeve inversely to that vol removes
almost all the crash and roughly doubles the strategy's Sharpe. It is not market
timing: the scalar is a function of the strategy's recent risk, not its direction.

research/momentum_risk.py measures exactly that for this system's 12-1 momentum
factor — reusing feat_momentum_12_1 so the risk model and the traded signal can
never diverge — and returns the target/realized exposure scalar and a crash-regime
flag (momentum vol in its own top decile). Pure functions, hand-verified against
computed values (§15): the scaling relationship, the leverage cap, the crash-tail
trip, and the long-short construction on a known panel.

Wired where it pays: run_all_studies computes it while the panel is already loaded
and caches it in one small row, and the autopilot reads that row to halve its
add-cap in the momentum crash regime — the one stress that specifically threatens
the momentum sleeve, distinct from broad VIX stress. The scalar is now a lever the
sizing layer can pull as the calibration record matures.
