Self-review (code-review, high) of the risk-managed-momentum integration surfaced
seven issues, all fixed:

1. The scalar only shrank the risk-budget path, so a position pinned at the 10%
   cap or by portfolio heat was barely reduced — the crash protection missed
   exactly the large, capped winners it targets. It now de-levers the FINAL
   exposure, after every constraint.
2. The cached crash flag had no freshness gate and consumers acted on it blind. A
   computed_at stamp now travels with the payload and a shared is_actionable()
   refuses a flag older than ~10 days; as_of is the panel's latest date, not the
   run date, matching the model's freshness convention.
3. `float(scalar or 1.0)` read an exposure scalar of exactly 0.0 as full size —
   the maximal-de-lever case inverted. Now an explicit None check.
4. A DB-commit failure while caching clobbered a successfully-computed result.
   Compute and cache-write are now separated; a hiccup fails to persist, never
   overwrites the run's result.
5. De-levering applied only to longs, leaving the short sleeve full-size in a
   crash — but momentum crashes are symmetric (the shorted losers rip up). Both
   legs are now scaled.
6. The de-risk message could render "None×"; guarded.
7. Redundant local imports replaced with the module-level ones.
